---
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { stats } from '../data/branches';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="About us"
  eyebrow="Our mission"
  lead="Science is something you do, not just something you read. Veylora exists to give every learner a free, hands-on science lab in their browser."
  description="Learn about Veylora, a free interactive science lab with simulations, explanations and quizzes for students, teachers and lifelong learners."
  ads
>
  <h2>Why we built {site.name}</h2>
  <p>
    Many of the most important ideas in science are invisible or hard to observe directly: water molecules crossing a
    membrane, electrons occupying energy levels, gravity bending the path of light. Textbooks describe these ideas with
    words and static diagrams. We think learners understand them better when they can change the conditions themselves
    and see what happens.
  </p>
  <p>
    {site.name} is a growing collection of interactive simulations. Each one focuses on a single concept and pairs the
    model with a clear explanation, the key terms and formulas, real-world applications, common misconceptions and a
    quiz with instant feedback.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Elementary and high school students</strong> exploring science for the first time or preparing for exams.</li>
    <li><strong>STEM, biology, nursing and medical students</strong> who want to visualize mechanisms they study in lectures.</li>
    <li><strong>Teachers</strong> looking for a demonstration they can project in class or assign for practice.</li>
    <li><strong>Lifelong learners</strong> who are simply curious about how the world works.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we build each simulator</h2>
  <p>
    Every simulator is a <em>simplified model</em>. We choose simplifications that keep the core science correct while
    making the model run smoothly on an ordinary laptop or phone. Where a model leaves something out, the page says so.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Educational content is written originally for {site.name} and checked against open textbooks and other reputable sources, which are listed on each simulator page.</li>
    <li>Anatomy and physiology content is presented strictly as education, never as medical advice. See our <a href="/disclaimer/">educational and medical disclaimer</a>.</li>
    <li>Every simulator has written instructions, keyboard-accessible controls and a reset button.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where we are today</h2>
  <p>
    The lab is organized into {stats.branches} branches of science with {stats.topics} planned topics. Simulators are
    published one at a time, only when they are complete — {stats.live === 1 ? 'one is' : `${stats.live} are`} live now,
    and the rest are clearly marked as in development.
  </p>

  <h2>Always free</h2>
  <p>
    All simulations and educational content on {site.name} are free to use, with no account required. To cover the cost
    of running and expanding the site, we may display advertising in the future. If we do, ads will be kept clearly
    separate from simulations and controls, and our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> will be updated before any ads
    appear.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    Found an error, have an idea for a simulator, or want to tell us how you use {site.name} in your classroom? We would
    love to hear from you on our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
